From f761fe0abcc6ec4c2a1b18265e4c8ad1a03c3cf4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Santosh Kumar Adhikari <santoshkumaradhikari@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 19:56:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix two claims in ch 0.3 flagged by fact-check

- Q4 (April-July) capex rush does not overlap Dashain: Dashain falls in
  Ashwin (late Sep-Oct), Q1 of the following fiscal year - corrected the
  seasonal table cell
- Phase 2 microfinance rule still cited the flat rate cap; aligned with
  the base-rate-plus-premium regime in force since Shrawan 2082, matching
  chapters 0.2/31/73/85
---
 investors-canon/book.html                              | 4 ++--
 investors-canon/chapters/ch-0-3-liquidity-regimes.html | 4 ++--
 2 files changed, 4 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/investors-canon/book.html b/investors-canon/book.html
index 3be9962..28c2f0f 100644
--- a/investors-canon/book.html
+++ b/investors-canon/book.html
@@ -759,7 +759,7 @@
     <tr><td>Priority action 2: Reduce margin-collateral-heavy stocks — these are first targets of forced selling.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Banking sector: Reduce to underweight. Avoid banks with high CD ratios and thin capital cushions.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Hydropower under construction: Reduce. COD delays during credit tightening are highly probable.</td></tr>
-    <tr><td>Microfinance: Exit or significantly reduce. Rate cap + rising funding costs = earnings compression.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Microfinance: Exit or significantly reduce. Regulated lending-rate ceiling (a capped premium over base rate since Shrawan 2082) + rising funding costs = lagged, squeezed margins.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Cash: Build to 40–60%. Capital preservation is the primary objective in Phase 2.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Do NOT: Buy on 'cheap' valuations during Phase 2 — the earnings denominator is still falling.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Exit trigger from Phase 2 monitoring: Two consecutive months of declining CD ratio AND falling interbank rates.</td></tr>
@@ -849,7 +849,7 @@
     <tr><td>Q1 — slow execution</td><td>August–October</td><td>Government deposits sterilising liquidity</td><td>System tighter than it appears</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Q2 — still slow</td><td>November–January</td><td>Continued fiscal drag on liquidity</td><td>CD ratio may appear tight; partially fiscal</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Q3 — spending acceleration</td><td>February–March</td><td>Liquidity injection from government payments</td><td>Positive for credit availability</td></tr>
-    <tr><td>Q4 — year-end rush</td><td>April–July</td><td>Large government outflows into private sector</td><td>Liquidity improves; overlaps with Dashain build-up</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Q4 — year-end rush</td><td>April–July</td><td>Large government outflows into private sector</td><td>Liquidity improves into fiscal year-end; Dashain cash demand follows in Q1 of the new year (Sep–Oct)</td></tr>
   </table>
   </div>
   <p>Table 0.3.6 — Government Capex Cycle and Liquidity Implications by Month</p>
diff --git a/investors-canon/chapters/ch-0-3-liquidity-regimes.html b/investors-canon/chapters/ch-0-3-liquidity-regimes.html
index 3a2fad0..b625cf2 100644
--- a/investors-canon/chapters/ch-0-3-liquidity-regimes.html
+++ b/investors-canon/chapters/ch-0-3-liquidity-regimes.html
@@ -161,7 +161,7 @@
     <tr><td>Priority action 2: Reduce margin-collateral-heavy stocks — these are first targets of forced selling.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Banking sector: Reduce to underweight. Avoid banks with high CD ratios and thin capital cushions.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Hydropower under construction: Reduce. COD delays during credit tightening are highly probable.</td></tr>
-    <tr><td>Microfinance: Exit or significantly reduce. Rate cap + rising funding costs = earnings compression.</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Microfinance: Exit or significantly reduce. Regulated lending-rate ceiling (a capped premium over base rate since Shrawan 2082) + rising funding costs = lagged, squeezed margins.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Cash: Build to 40–60%. Capital preservation is the primary objective in Phase 2.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Do NOT: Buy on 'cheap' valuations during Phase 2 — the earnings denominator is still falling.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Exit trigger from Phase 2 monitoring: Two consecutive months of declining CD ratio AND falling interbank rates.</td></tr>
@@ -251,7 +251,7 @@
     <tr><td>Q1 — slow execution</td><td>August–October</td><td>Government deposits sterilising liquidity</td><td>System tighter than it appears</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Q2 — still slow</td><td>November–January</td><td>Continued fiscal drag on liquidity</td><td>CD ratio may appear tight; partially fiscal</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Q3 — spending acceleration</td><td>February–March</td><td>Liquidity injection from government payments</td><td>Positive for credit availability</td></tr>
-    <tr><td>Q4 — year-end rush</td><td>April–July</td><td>Large government outflows into private sector</td><td>Liquidity improves; overlaps with Dashain build-up</td></tr>
+    <tr><td>Q4 — year-end rush</td><td>April–July</td><td>Large government outflows into private sector</td><td>Liquidity improves into fiscal year-end; Dashain cash demand follows in Q1 of the new year (Sep–Oct)</td></tr>
   </table>
   </div>
   <p>Table 0.3.6 — Government Capex Cycle and Liquidity Implications by Month</p>
